WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:01.110
Good Morning Hank, It's Monday.

00:00:01.110 --> 00:00:04.920
I spent much of this weekend in New York's
Central Park, and for some reason I kept thinking

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:06.420
of my favorite photograph.

00:00:06.420 --> 00:00:10.839
So there was this German photographer, August
Sander, who's famous for his massive, never-finished

00:00:10.839 --> 00:00:15.240
series "People of the 20th Century" in which
he took pictures of everyone from bricklayers

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:18.180
to circus performers to famous composers.

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:22.570
Along the way he also took my favorite picture
- this one - which is sometimes known as "Three

00:00:22.570 --> 00:00:25.640
farmers on their way to a dance - 1914".

00:00:25.640 --> 00:00:27.240
There's a lot I like about this picture.

00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:29.760
I like how the farmer's heads are cut off
by the horizon.

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:33.720
I like how their faces are in the sky but
their feet are in the mud, and I like what

00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:38.730
the photograph tells us about class and history;
that by 1914 mass production meant that these

00:00:38.730 --> 00:00:42.270
young farmers could afford suits and fancy
hats and canes.

00:00:42.270 --> 00:00:47.230
For the first time in history, European peasants
in the countryside could dress like, or almost

00:00:47.230 --> 00:00:48.230
like, urban professionals.

00:00:48.230 --> 00:00:51.340
But then again they are still walking instead
of driving in a car.

00:00:51.340 --> 00:00:55.649
These are young men on the cusp of what I
think of as the contemporary world and I like

00:00:55.649 --> 00:00:59.680
how they're looking over their shoulders as
if they can only briefly pause for the camera

00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:02.540
before they head off to their futures - to
the dance.

00:01:02.540 --> 00:01:06.580
It was the middle of 1914 and these three
farmers were, of course, on their way to two

00:01:06.580 --> 00:01:10.040
dances - the one they knew about, and World
War One.

00:01:10.040 --> 00:01:13.720
These men very likely ended up fighting in
that war, and they may well have been among

00:01:13.720 --> 00:01:16.240
the 17-million people who died in it.

00:01:16.240 --> 00:01:20.320
And the very same industrial manufacturing
innovations that made their clothes affordable,

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:24.660
created the bullets and machine guns that
made WWI so lethal.

00:01:24.660 --> 00:01:27.500
The three people in this photograph are living
in the middle of history.

00:01:27.500 --> 00:01:32.560
Like, to us, the dance they were walking towards
seems inevitable but to see their faces is

00:01:32.560 --> 00:01:34.190
to know that it wasn't.

00:01:34.190 --> 00:01:38.650
That history is choices we -collectively-
make about how to look at the world and how

00:01:38.650 --> 00:01:40.500
to respond to what we see.

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:44.780
These three young farmers, walking along a
road in the German Vestervald 101 years ago

00:01:44.780 --> 00:01:49.720
remind me that I am also in the middle of
history and that how I imagine the world,

00:01:49.720 --> 00:01:52.630
and the people in it, really matters.

00:01:52.630 --> 00:01:56.750
Someone once told me that "photographs are
just light and time" and in this picture,

00:01:56.750 --> 00:02:00.610
August Sander captured a helluva light and
a helluva time.

00:02:00.610 --> 00:02:04.592
I pray we never see the likes of it again.

00:02:04.592 --> 00:02:04.819
Hank, I'll see you tomorrow.

